                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "dual-voice":</h3><p><b>Dual-voice</b> microphones provide two or more distinct voices or timbres within one microphone assembly &#8212; without changing any parts. Mic designers employ a variety of methods of achieving multiple voices from a single mic: dual-sided transducers in which each side is tuned differently, active or passive switchable EQ circuits, polarization voltage changes, etc.</p><p>MXL uses a dual-sided capsule in which the front and rear membranes are of a slightly different composition (8-micron vs. 6-micron Mylar), and are likely tuned differently. There might be differences in the two halves of the capsule backplate, as well. Selection of front or rear sides is done via a &#8220;warm/bright&#8221; switch, and of course requires that the mic be rotated so that the active side of the capsule (indicated by an LED inside the headbasket) faces the sound source.</p><p>The Blue Microphones Spark has a &#8220;focus&#8221; switch that enables both a high-pass filter as well as other sonic changes that as of this writing have not been fully described. One of the tricks embodied in the Focus switch &#8212; for which a patent has been applied &#8212; is a change to the capsule&#8217;s polarization voltage.</p><p>The Miktek M7 has a similar feature.</p><p>Numerous ribbon microphones from AEA, Royer Labs, and Crowley &amp; Tripp (now Shure) use a variety of techniques to produce distinct timbres from the front and back of a bidirectional ribbon element.</p><p>A few other mics, notably the Chameleon Labs TS-2 and TS-1 Mk II, use a variable heater voltage control to alter the tonal qualities of the tube amplifier circuit.</p>
